\par \f1\b\fs28 3. Your data and local custody\par
\f0\b0\fs24
\par
You retain your rights in the content Hippocampus processes for you. The local\par
database, extracted text, embeddings, and captured artifacts remain on your Mac\par
unless you direct another tool to receive them.\par
\par
The database is encrypted with SQLCipher. The database key is stored in a\par
non-synchronizing macOS Keychain item for use by the shipped local components.\par
This design reduces exposure but does not make the key non-exportable and does\par
not protect against every process running as the same macOS user. Backups,\par
exports, external tools, and copies you create are outside the application's\par
local deletion boundary.\par
\par
\par \f1\b\fs28 4. Capture controls and sensitive content\par
\f0\b0\fs24
\par
Screen capture is off by default and requires macOS Screen Recording permission\par
plus an explicit in-app setting. Hippocampus includes controls intended to\par
exclude or suppress sensitive surfaces. Those controls can miss content. You\par
are responsible for reviewing capture settings, blocked applications, and the\par
content visible on your screen.\par
\par
\par \f1\b\fs28 5. Retention and deletion\par
\f0\b0\fs24
\par
When a retention rule expires or you delete a memory, Hippocampus removes the\par
corresponding database rows and compacts local database storage. Compaction can\par
reclaim database space, but deletion is not a guarantee of forensic erasure\par
from storage media, backups, exports, or copies held by other tools.\par
\par
To remove the local product and its primary data:\par
\par
1. Quit Hippocampus.\par
2. Delete the Hippocampus application.\par
3. Delete ~/Library/Application Support/MCI/.\par
4. Optionally delete ~/Library/Logs/MCI/.\par
\par
\par \f1\b\fs28 6. Security and availability limitations\par
\f0\b0\fs24
\par
No software security control is perfect. Hippocampus does not warrant that\par
capture filtering will identify every sensitive surface, that OCR or search\par
results will be complete or accurate, that encryption will be free of\par
vulnerabilities, or that the software will operate without interruption or data\par
loss. Keep backups appropriate for your needs and protect access to your Mac\par
account.\par
